{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $8x-4y<12?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-4y & <12 \\\\ -4y & <-8x+12 \\\\ 4y & >8x-12 \\\\ y & >2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=2x-3.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>2x-3$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/d399f186d02b777d8b6774833cd9e0855de5f228b1e6872027d7909d2e6dbe6f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-64020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/06a904255ae51542be4dd3bab0edf54bf9d3cc3cd32a2ae07f442e5114db46ce.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/6f80afd5890da875ba16684156e673f3a368904b11c056a72841cf6237ccab21.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/c7892faf71691acb91e82a78f43df86797bade59037d25746f88f93a7fde55f2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/e68e5e19e2ed63a4f9c1b65f8af7bed53568d304c3b0ce916617f1f305db3b2c.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $4x-2y<6?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & <6 \\\\ -2y & <-4x+6 \\\\ 2y & >4x-6 \\\\ y & >2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=2x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>2x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2fe69dace2487c1b6dc73fcd122fd358513d8ac9528b3195d2075f8b62c75b4a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1497c291a9a068a414503d3081f52c5df56019d496a3f6c16cc905e6a20b97fe.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1cc25938438d7ef2ad77bd5047a1417e5c0dba609e630bde7c3dee9dffa205ba.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/9e6af7aceed6e31bccd5f568d50ac1c897b98cc568d383ea559a8b61abc203e7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/9febb7b2ffb18d3d4d1ea5976fb0de6ee164689d01d82cf89dfb83000febffb1.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x-y<3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & <3 \\\\ -y & <-x+3 \\\\ y & >x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/77a4c9b6bc705a2fbf486bfef5a6ce57924aae86c4abf1510040c7957b873329.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/00b6630417457321be388e1a95f333bda2307e4730e8e319b9aaca1b956f10d9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/06a8431298502b6f05ac9105d1f3a8ce3a18c4d3fddfca03c3d2590cf5c3d26a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2f84a880846617baf3adfa1fda9ddf397656a0760ca4f26d53a94346dce65bf7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/62cf58531b676c4b08315ca1a70efa68e128215bf114c40f304295f34517af6d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/d66507624f9d90b4b774e75bb62905f0675c1cf14a5881680c2fcfd6ef45cf36.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64021" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/d66507624f9d90b4b774e75bb62905f0675c1cf14a5881680c2fcfd6ef45cf36.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $5x-10y<-20?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10y & <-20 \\\\ -10y & <-5x-20 \\\\ 10y & >5x+20 \\\\ y & >\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/351bb4b4324432e2cc747e67dac22100da3c9e5318339832efa046f504394c35.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/0668624761edfaf6f67a69d6059e7b0c54acd87042ec9f7400f1d4c2dfc5ff0b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1e408923db2c7223715b9f82a2777a411e2c79b3614320975c237f2f503f2368.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/458a5dd519867a662237d7b52e44627d1867f25dcceeea446203a42571f91a00.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a16d99b349797e305083d8295ef098fbfbe3ea82970b5a48606ea5c678e08262.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/ebf7fd57f5d07afebc22899418ce34d14e59dcdb2a01087950de675b7086f405.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-109942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a16d99b349797e305083d8295ef098fbfbe3ea82970b5a48606ea5c678e08262.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x-y>-5?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >-5 \\\\ -y & >-x-5 \\\\ y & <x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=x+5.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=x+5$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y<x+5$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y=x+5,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/bd0ce52fe5aaf5e95c4f2eb5bd14652ee696c7bb2dfdb99ccdf07543be46f45e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/0541476ba87e303dd3209dd6ec5cb848dcf965fc82d42ca93c91f0865bb7de9c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/9c03e15abc255bcc4fecf6ba6f617639ee06e2720c81891a936ba906ae6c83f2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a50effb3acd9884ab7f351a820720a0a3ab7728c9242c576a17ddc58ad2d7111.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/baba27c5989981679ac5e5290db3b88803f892659869b54991b17a0b1574e422.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/c60ad7f7e7e4f801102359920f17320370657445ab6d3cd58865184dd8bcdb28.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-64023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/baba27c5989981679ac5e5290db3b88803f892659869b54991b17a0b1574e422.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $2x+4y\\ge 8?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y & \\ge 8 \\\\ 4y & \\ge -2x+8 \\\\ y & \\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/226415c7de8240eaa2a87a10e87d95c6dbf45df6263c4b36ea819cfec097a8a0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2a46c79189ad438d1c4bd1528e50e93881bbf7690d37b16b9ebeaaa44bff06a8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/35e2d168ece734f2bc84a3b9c41208cb941fabbf8b4c9b1de600f8c5dad5ac1d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/5456e2ee349d219f3072d305415c939d5d4a3b0cbc189b55be3102fb5c72a3a7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/5f7df5dc434ecc1a04fc60aa476303db2ccea9dda6b8a9fa28eae5dedddc44b6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8e7f4a75c7793e4770643cc40decbfd007f7809f96475a914c5c00d4f5ee13bc.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8e7f4a75c7793e4770643cc40decbfd007f7809f96475a914c5c00d4f5ee13bc.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x+2y\\le 4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-142112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & \\le 4 \\\\ 2y & \\le -x+4 \\\\ y & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**. \n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\le -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8b906cf167528f484109528a93732dc2b8d73558bb02f454bb520af87efde3c5.png)