-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-44558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/daa0365127a12a9a561d0790af855b5f26f8ab9188ac541b142cd9d9e0a919cc.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-44558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The border-line is a horizontal line passing through the point $(0,2).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $y=2.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal to\" inequality. Since the shaded region is *above* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than or equal to* those on the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-44558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-44558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b9642