a256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-215437" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\le -1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/dcfffb2c9005da34a529cdf5ef372bdac840a1308b92507ad0ea0a5359a1387f.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-4,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-4.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx>-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b460bba77719b4f78be355ada84d06e8ca0a4e1640f504f293bd45d88f7dac64"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x>-4"] ["math" "x\\ge -4"] ["math" "y<x-4"] ["math" "y>-4"] ["math" "y\\le -4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "q-29423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x>-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/state.json" "file_sha256" "47b94d1f8c373ef374e750558c0cbdc64980888e0614b964231a50eb4e3186de"} {"question" "e-14041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $x+2y < 4$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14041.json" "file_sha256" "6f1e6d27b91da89bda7118c9b50b54cd6c98efc2fec03ace61843b7bf0e0d32f"} {"question" "e-14041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & <4 \\\\ 2y & <-x+4 \\\\ y & <-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14041.json" "file_sha256" "6f1e6d27b91da89bda7118c9b50b54cd6c98efc2fec03ace61843b7bf0e0d32f"} {"question" "e-14047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $-x-y \\leq -1$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14047.json" "file_sha256" "eea1c8294a776ef8e1033bb07028e4d08ad678384d5baf5dc889f0147e470aff"} {"question" "e-14047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-y & \\le -1 \\\\ -y & \\le x-1 \\\\ y & \\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-x+1.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-x+1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-x+1,$ so we shade *above* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14047.json" "file_sha256" "eea1c8294a776ef8e1033bb07028e4d08ad678384d5baf5dc889f0147e470aff"} {"question" "e-14056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inequality that describes the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/677e2257e2cb3a5706b7dc2d1088fc68ff12a2c39d976819d52f25fb2dfbe77d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14056.json" "file_sha256" "d78cfcf118d7eec94b4eeeb8f11e017768bb5e1e9cfb97605560ab57f816572b"} {"question" "e-14056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation \n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. \n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx > -1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/example-14056.json" "file_sha256" "d78cfcf118d7eec94b4eeeb8f11e017768bb5e1e9cfb97605560ab57f816572b"}] "usage" {"q-29423" [] "e-14041" [] "q-64021" [] "e-14056" [] "e-14047" []} "basis" 1068 "retractions" [[17592186184763 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x - y < 3$?"] [17592186184679 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/q-29423.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above."] [17592186183885 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y & < 4 \\\\ 2y & < - x + 4 \\\\ y & < - \\frac{1}{2}x + 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nitself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nso we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/eea9bbe3f74a8d4df15c497ee51991b6.png)"] [17592186183886 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x - y & \\le - 1 \\\\ -y & \\le x - 1 \\\\ y & \\ge - x + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nitself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nso we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/c0cf262705b842b59fc8f287c310b7c8.png)"] [17592186183888 :question/problem "Find the inequality that describes the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/43fad45f1d96c5e8626ab609a68bfd59.png)"] [17592186183888 :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0)$. Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1$.\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use.\n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n$x > -1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186182969 17592186182970 17592186182971 17592186182972 17592186182973 17592186183468 17592186183469 17592186183470 17592186183471 17592186183472] "immutable_fields" [[17592186182968 :answer-field/correct] [17592186182968 :answer-field/id] [17592186182968 :answer-field/key] [17592186182968 :answer-field/type] [17592186183467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183467 :answer-field/id] [17592186183467 :answer-field/key] [17592186183467 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
